2. The Research Problem: Beyond State-Centric Intervention

2.1 The Conventional Intervention Model

A conventional intervention model in systems science and policy analysis can be expressed as:

S_{t+1} = F(S_t, A_t, \xi_t)

where:

· S_t denotes the system state at time t;
· A_t denotes an intervention or action;
· \xi_t denotes uncertainty or environmental disturbances;
· F denotes the transformation mechanism.

This representation is useful for stable mechanical or engineered systems. It is fundamentally incomplete for complex human systems, where the transformation mechanism itself is subject to modification through the very processes it governs.

2.2 The Static-Mechanism Fallacy

Complex human systems systematically violate the assumption of mechanism stability. Institutions change their rules. Networks reconfigure their structures. Goals evolve through learning and political contestation. Actors acquire new knowledge and modify their behavior. New technologies expand the space of available actions. New information changes perceptions of problems and possibilities.

Therefore:

F_t \neq F_{t+1}

represents a fundamental property of complex human systems rather than an exceptional circumstance. The mechanism governing system evolution is itself an evolving object.

2.3 The Generalized Representation

This observation leads to a generalized representation:

S_{t+1} = F_t(S_t, \mathcal{T}_t, \xi_t)

together with:

F_{t+1} = \Omega(F_t, K_t, \mathcal{T}_t, L_t, E_t)

where:

· K_t denotes the system's transformation capability at time t;
· L_t denotes learning processes;
· E_t denotes accumulated experience;
· \Omega denotes the transformation of the transformation mechanism itself.

2.4 The Central Conceptual Transition

This produces the central conceptual transition of ASTF:

\boxed{\text{State Transformation} \rightarrow \text{Transformation-System Transformation}}

The framework moves beyond the question "How can we change the system?" to ask "How can we change the system's capacity to change itself?"

