3. Intellectual Foundations and Positioning

3.1 Relationship to Existing Theories

ASTF is not proposed as a replacement for existing theories but as an integration layer. However, to establish its novel contribution, it must be explicitly distinguished from related concepts.

3.1.1 Dynamic Capabilities

Teece, Pisano, and Shuen (1997) define dynamic capabilities as "the firm's ability to integrate, build, and reconfigure internal and external competences to address rapidly changing environments." This concept focuses on firm-level competitive advantage through sensing, seizing, and transforming.

ASTF Difference: Dynamic capabilities operate within a given transformation mechanism. ASTF models the evolution of the mechanism itself. ASTF's K_t is a systemic property spanning multiple agents, institutions, and network structures, not a property of a single organization.

3.1.2 Absorptive Capacity

Cohen and Levinthal (1990) define absorptive capacity as the ability to recognize the value of new information, assimilate it, and apply it to commercial ends.

ASTF Difference: Absorptive capacity is a subset of K_t—specifically, components k_o (observation) and k_l (learning). ASTF extends beyond information absorption to include structural reconfiguration (k_s), rule revision (k_r), goal revision (k_g), and meta-transformation (k_x). Moreover, absorptive capacity does not model the evolution of the adaptation mechanism itself.

3.1.3 Resilience and Adaptability

Resilience theory (Holling, 1973; Walker et al., 2004) distinguishes between resilience as the capacity to absorb disturbance, adaptability as the capacity to adjust responses to changing external drivers, and transformability as "the capacity to create a fundamentally new system when ecological, economic, or social structures make the existing system untenable."

ASTF Difference: ASTF treats transformability not as a separate concept but as a structured, multi-dimensional capability K_t that can be measured, compared across systems, and deliberately enhanced. Furthermore, ASTF models transformability itself as an evolving property—a system can become more or less transformable over time through its own operations.

3.1.4 Organizational Learning and Meta-Learning

Argyris and Schön (1978) distinguish single-loop learning (adjusting actions), double-loop learning (adjusting governing variables), and deutero-learning (learning how to learn). Schmidhuber (1987) and Thrun and Pratt (1998) formalize meta-learning in computational contexts as learning about learning processes.

ASTF Difference: ASTF generalizes these concepts to a multi-layer system representation. The learning loops in ASTF operate not only on cognitive categories but on institutional rules, network structures, and the transformation operator itself. The formalization through parameterized operators provides a unifying mathematical language.

