4. The Three-Layer Dynamical Architecture

4.1 The Core Structure

ASTF is fundamentally a three-layer dynamical theory:

\boxed{
\begin{array}{c}
\text{System State } S_t \\
\updownarrow \\
\text{Transformation Capability } K_t \\
\updownarrow \\
\text{Transformation Mechanism } \theta_t
\end{array}}

The coupled dynamics are:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
S_{t+1} &= f(S_t, \mathcal{T}_t; \theta_t) + \xi_t \\
K_{t+1} &= \kappa(K_t, \mathcal{T}_t, L_t, E_t, \theta_t) \\
\theta_{t+1} &= g(\theta_t, S_t, A_t, I_t, L_t)
\end{aligned}}

where:

· f is the state transition function;
· \kappa is the capability update function;
· g is the mechanism update function;
· \theta_t parameterizes the current transformation mechanism;
· \xi_t is environmental disturbance.

4.2 The Multi-Layer System Model

The full multi-layer representation is:

\mathcal{M}_t = (S_t, A_t, I_t, R_t, N_t, G_t, K_t, C_t, E_t)

where:

Layer Symbol Description
State S_t Measurable condition of the system at time t
Agent A_t Decision-making entities
Information I_t Data, knowledge, observations, models
Rules R_t Formal and informal constraints governing behavior
Network N_t Structure of relationships and connections
Goals G_t Objectives guiding system behavior
Capability K_t Capacity to observe, learn, adapt, transform
Resources C_t Available resources for transformation
Environment E_t External conditions and disturbances

4.3 The Complete System State

The complete system state is:

\boxed{X_t = (S_t, K_t, \theta_t)}

This compressed representation captures the essential dynamics: state, capability, and mechanism co-evolve.

4.4 The Three Orders of Adaptation

The three-layer architecture yields a clean distinction:

\boxed{\text{First-order adaptation: change the system.}}

\boxed{\text{Second-order adaptation: change how the system changes.}}

\boxed{\text{Meta-adaptation: change how the system learns to change how it changes.}}

These distinctions correspond to:

Order Mathematical Object Description
First-order S_t \rightarrow S_{t+1} State transformation
Second-order \theta_t \rightarrow \theta_{t+1} Mechanism transformation
Meta-adaptation g \rightarrow g' Learning about mechanism transformation

