10. Transformation as a Control Problem

10.1 The Refined Objective

ASTF proposes that the system seeks to maximize a transformation objective. To avoid double-counting capability value, the objective is defined over the augmented state X_t = (S_t, K_t, \theta_t):

\boxed{\pi^* = \arg\max_{\pi} \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{t=0}^{T} \gamma^t \, r(X_t, \mathcal{T}_t) \right]}

where r(X_t, \mathcal{T}_t) is the reward function, \gamma \in (0,1) is the discount factor, and \pi = \{\mathcal{T}_0, \mathcal{T}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{T}_{T-1}\} is the transformation policy.

10.2 The Constrained Optimization

The optimization is subject to constraints:

C(\pi) \leq C_{\max}

R_T(\pi) \leq R_{\max}

\mathcal{T}_{\text{feasibility}}(\pi) = 1

\text{Inequality}(\pi) \leq I_{\max}

\text{Irreversibility}(\pi) \leq Q_{\max}

10.3 The Bellman Formulation

Under the Markov assumption:

V(X_t) = \max_{\mathcal{T}_t} \left[ r(X_t, \mathcal{T}_t) + \gamma \, \mathbb{E}[V(X_{t+1}) \mid X_t, \mathcal{T}_t] \right]

This formulation naturally captures the capability dividend: actions that increase K_t are valued through their effect on future V(X_{t+1}).

10.4 The Optimal Transformation Principle

\boxed{\text{Optimal Transformation} = \arg\max_{\mathcal T} \left[ \text{long-term systemic value} + \text{future capability value} - \text{risk} - \text{cost} \right]}

This principle rejects the assumption that deeper transformation is always better. Instead, transformation is evaluated according to its net contribution to long-term systemic value, accounting for both immediate gains and future capability, while constraining risk and cost.

