APPENDIX B

Mechanism Stability: Complete Proofs and Sufficient Conditions

B.1 The Mechanism Space

Definition B.1.1 (Parameter Space): The transformation mechanism is parameterized by \theta \in \Theta, where \Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p is a compact convex subset of \mathbb{R}^p. The space \Theta is equipped with the Euclidean metric:

d_\Theta(\theta_1, \theta_2) = \|\theta_1 - \theta_2\|_2

Since \Theta is compact and \mathbb{R}^p is complete, (\Theta, d_\Theta) is a complete metric space.

Definition B.1.2 (Input Space): The non-mechanism inputs are \mathbf{x} = (S, A, I, L) \in \mathcal{X}, where \mathcal{X} is a compact subset of a product of state spaces. The space \mathcal{X} is equipped with the product metric:

d_{\mathcal{X}}(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2) = \|S_1 - S_2\| + \|A_1 - A_2\| + \|I_1 - I_2\| + \|L_1 - L_2\|

B.2 The Mechanism Update Function

Definition B.2.1 (Mechanism Update Function): The mechanism update function is:

g: \Theta \times \mathcal{X} \rightarrow \Theta

mapping the current mechanism parameter and system inputs to the next mechanism parameter:

\theta_{t+1} = g(\theta_t, \mathbf{x}_t)

B.3 Sufficient Conditions for Contraction

Assumption B.3.1 (Lipschitz Conditions): There exist constants \beta \in [0, 1) and \gamma \geq 0 such that for all \theta_1, \theta_2 \in \Theta and \mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2 \in \mathcal{X}:

\|g(\theta_1, \mathbf{x}_1) - g(\theta_2, \mathbf{x}_2)\| \leq \beta \|\theta_1 - \theta_2\| + \gamma \|\mathbf{x}_1 - \mathbf{x}_2\|

Interpretation:

· \beta < 1: The mechanism update is "forgetful" or "regularizing" in the mechanism parameter. Differences in the current mechanism are dampened rather than amplified.
· \gamma \geq 0: The mechanism update is Lipschitz in the system inputs. The mechanism responds continuously to changes in the system state, agent actions, information, and learning.

Proposition B.3.1 (Sufficient Condition for \beta < 1): If g is differentiable and:

\sup_{\theta, \mathbf{x}} \left\| \frac{\partial g}{\partial \theta}(\theta, \mathbf{x}) \right\|_{\text{op}} \leq \beta < 1

where \|\cdot\|_{\text{op}} denotes the operator norm, then g satisfies the contraction property in \theta.

Proof: By the mean value theorem for vector-valued functions, for any \theta_1, \theta_2 \in \Theta and fixed \mathbf{x}:

g(\theta_1, \mathbf{x}) - g(\theta_2, \mathbf{x}) = \left[ \int_0^1 \frac{\partial g}{\partial \theta}(\theta_2 + t(\theta_1 - \theta_2), \mathbf{x}) \, dt \right] (\theta_1 - \theta_2)

Taking norms:

\|g(\theta_1, \mathbf{x}) - g(\theta_2, \mathbf{x})\| \leq \left\| \int_0^1 \frac{\partial g}{\partial \theta}(\theta_2 + t(\theta_1 - \theta_2), \mathbf{x}) \, dt \right\|_{\text{op}} \|\theta_1 - \theta_2\|

Since the operator norm of the integral is bounded by the integral of the operator norms:

\left\| \int_0^1 \frac{\partial g}{\partial \theta}(\theta_2 + t(\theta_1 - \theta_2), \mathbf{x}) \, dt \right\|_{\text{op}} \leq \int_0^1 \left\| \frac{\partial g}{\partial \theta}(\theta_2 + t(\theta_1 - \theta_2), \mathbf{x}) \right\|_{\text{op}} dt \leq \beta

Therefore:

\|g(\theta_1, \mathbf{x}) - g(\theta_2, \mathbf{x})\| \leq \beta \|\theta_1 - \theta_2\|

\square

B.4 The Mechanism Stability Theorem

Theorem B.4.1 (Mechanism Stability): Suppose Assumption B.3.1 holds with \beta < 1. Then for any fixed input sequence \{\mathbf{x}_t\}_{t=0}^{\infty}, the iteration:

\theta_{t+1} = g(\theta_t, \mathbf{x}_t)

converges to a unique trajectory \{\theta_t^*\}_{t=0}^{\infty} that depends continuously on \{\mathbf{x}_t\}.

Proof:

For fixed \mathbf{x}_t, define the map:

G_t(\theta) = g(\theta, \mathbf{x}_t)

By Assumption B.3.1, G_t is a contraction with factor \beta < 1. By the Banach Fixed-Point Theorem, G_t has a unique fixed point \theta_t^*:

\theta_t^* = G_t(\theta_t^*) = g(\theta_t^*, \mathbf{x}_t)

Moreover, for any initial \theta_0, the sequence \{\theta_t\} defined by \theta_{t+1} = G_t(\theta_t) converges to the fixed point at a rate:

\|\theta_t - \theta_t^*\| \leq \frac{\beta^t}{1-\beta} \|\theta_0 - \theta_0^*\|

For continuous dependence on \{\mathbf{x}_t\}, let \{\mathbf{x}_t\} and \{\mathbf{x}_t'\} be two input sequences with:

\sup_t \|\mathbf{x}_t - \mathbf{x}_t'\| \leq \delta

Let \{\theta_t^*\} and \{\theta_t'^*\} be the corresponding fixed-point trajectories. Then:

\begin{aligned}
\|\theta_t^* - \theta_t'^*\| &= \|g(\theta_t^*, \mathbf{x}_t) - g(\theta_t'^*, \mathbf{x}_t')\| \\
&\leq \|g(\theta_t^*, \mathbf{x}_t) - g(\theta_t^*, \mathbf{x}_t')\| + \|g(\theta_t^*, \mathbf{x}_t') - g(\theta_t'^*, \mathbf{x}_t')\| \\
&\leq \gamma \, \delta + \beta \, \|\theta_t^* - \theta_t'^*\|
\end{aligned}

Therefore:

\|\theta_t^* - \theta_t'^*\| \leq \frac{\gamma \delta}{1-\beta}

which tends to 0 as \delta \rightarrow 0.

\square

B.5 The Induced Operator on Function Space

Definition B.5.1 (Induced Operator): Given the mechanism update function g, the induced operator \Omega_\theta on the space of mechanism parameter sequences is:

\Omega_\theta(\{\theta_t\}) = \{g(\theta_t, \mathbf{x}_t)\}_{t=0}^{\infty}

Theorem B.5.1 (Contraction on Sequence Space): If g satisfies Assumption B.3.1 with \beta < 1, then \Omega_\theta is a contraction on the space of bounded
 sequences \ell^\infty(\Theta) with contraction factor \beta.

Proof: For any two sequences \{\theta_t\} and \{\theta_t'\}:

\begin{aligned}
d_{\ell^\infty}(\Omega_\theta(\{\theta_t\}), \Omega_\theta(\{\theta_t'\})) &= \sup_t \|g(\theta_t, \mathbf{x}_t) - g(\theta_t', \mathbf{x}_t)\| \\
&\leq \sup_t \beta \|\theta_t - \theta_t'\| \\
&= \beta \, d_{\ell^\infty}(\{\theta_t\}, \{\theta_t'\})
\end{aligned}

\square

B.6 Interpretation for ASTF

The Mechanism Stability Theorem provides the formal guarantee that meta-transformation does not lead to runaway self-modification when \beta < 1. The parameter \beta becomes an empirical quantity that can be estimated from observed mechanism evolution:

\beta = \sup_{\theta \neq \theta'} \frac{\|g(\theta, \mathbf{x}) - g(\theta', \mathbf{x})\|}{\|\theta - \theta'\|}

Systems with \beta \ll 1 stabilize quickly; systems with \beta \rightarrow 1 exhibit prolonged meta-transition periods; systems with \beta > 1 may exhibit instability.

