APPENDIX C

Transformation Depth: Complete Normalization and Metric Properties

C.1 Component 1: Normalized Graph Edit Distance

Definition C.1.1 (Graph Edit Distance): For two graphs G_1 = (V_1, E_1) and G_2 = (V_2, E_2), the graph edit distance d_G(G_1, G_2) is the minimum total cost of elementary operations (node insertion, node deletion, edge insertion, edge deletion) required to transform G_1 into G_2.

Definition C.1.2 (Hamming Approximation): For graphs with the same node set V (i.e., |V_1| = |V_2| = n), the Hamming approximation is:

d_G(G_1, G_2) \approx \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} |A_1(i,j) - A_2(i,j)|

where A_1 and A_2 are the adjacency matrices.

Theorem C.1.1 (Maximum Hamming Distance): For simple graphs (no self-loops) with n nodes, the maximum Hamming distance between two adjacency matrices is:

d_G^{\max} = n(n-1)

Proof: Each entry A(i,j) with i \neq j can differ by at most 1 (since entries are in \{0, 1\}). There are n(n-1) off-diagonal entries. Self-loops are excluded by the simple graph assumption. Therefore:

d_G^{\max} = \sum_{i \neq j} 1 = n(n-1)

\square

Definition C.1.3 (Normalized Graph Edit Distance):

d_1 = \frac{d_G(G_t, G_{t+1})}{d_G^{\max}} = \frac{\sum_{i,j} |A_{t+1}(i,j) - A_t(i,j)|}{n(n-1)}

Proposition C.1.1: d_1 \in [0, 1].

Proof: The numerator is non-negative and bounded above by n(n-1) by the maximum Hamming distance theorem. Therefore d_1 \in [0, 1].

\square

C.2 Component 2: Normalized Rule-Set Entropy Change

Definition C.2.1 (Rule-Set Entropy): For a rule set R_t = \{r_1, r_2, \ldots, r_n\}, the Shannon entropy is:

H(R_t) = -\sum_{i=1}^{n} p(r_i) \ln p(r_i)

where p(r_i) is the relative frequency or weight of rule r_i.

Definition C.2.2 (Maximum Entropy): For n rules, the maximum entropy occurs when all rules are equally weighted:

H_{\max}(R_t) = \ln n

Definition C.2.3 (Normalized Entropy Change): To avoid division by zero:

d_2 = \frac{|H(R_{t+1}) - H(R_t)|}{\max(H(R_t), H_{\min})}

where H_{\min} > 0 is a small constant. Alternatively:

d_2 = \frac{|H(R_{t+1}) - H(R_t)|}{H_{\max}(R_t)} = \frac{|H(R_{t+1}) - H(R_t)|}{\ln |R_t|}

Proposition C.2.1: The alternative definition d_2 = |H(R_{t+1}) - H(R_t)| / \ln |R_t| satisfies d_2 \in [0, 1] when |R_t| \geq 2.

Proof: The maximum possible entropy change is bounded by \ln |R_t| (the maximum entropy of the current rule set). The minimum change is 0. Therefore:

0 \leq \frac{|H(R_{t+1}) - H(R_t)|}{\ln |R_t|} \leq \frac{\ln |R_t|}{\ln |R_t|} = 1

This assumes |R_t| \geq 2. For |R_t| = 1, H(R_t) = 0 and H_{\max} = 0, so the alternative definition is undefined. The original definition with H_{\min} is preferred in this case.

\square

C.3 Component 3: Normalized Capability Vector Shift

Definition C.3.1 (Capability Vector Shift): The capability vector shift is:

\Delta K_t = K_{t+1} - K_t

Definition C.3.2 (Normalized Capability Shift):

d_3 = \frac{\|\Delta K_t\|}{\|K_t\| + \varepsilon_K}

where \varepsilon_K > 0 is a small regularization constant.

Proposition C.3.1: d_3 \in [0, \frac{\|K_{t+1}\| + \|K_t\|}{\|K_t\| + \varepsilon_K}]. When \|K_{t+1}\| \leq \|K_t\| (capability does not increase), d_3 \leq 1 for sufficiently large \varepsilon_K.

Proof: By the triangle inequality:

\|\Delta K_t\| = \|K_{t+1} - K_t\| \leq \|K_{t+1}\| + \|K_t\|

Therefore:

d_3 \leq \frac{\|K_{t+1}\| + \|K_t\|}{\|K_t\| + \varepsilon_K}

If \|K_{t+1}\| \leq \|K_t\|, then:

d_3 \leq \frac{2\|K_t\|}{\|K_t\| + \varepsilon_K} \leq 2

For d_3 \leq 1, we need \varepsilon_K \geq \|K_t\|. The regularization constant should be chosen accordingly.

\square

Recommendation: Set \varepsilon_K = \kappa_{\text{typical}}, where \kappa_{\text{typical}} is the typical capability norm in the system. This ensures d_3 \leq 1 for most practical interventions.

C.4 Composite Transformation Depth

Definition C.4.1 (Composite Transformation Depth):

D(\mathcal{T}) = \min\left(1, \, w_1 d_1 + w_2 d_2 + w_3 d_3\right)

where w_1 + w_2 + w_3 = 1, w_i \geq 0.

Theorem C.4.1 (Boundedness): D(\mathcal{T}) \in [0, 1].

Proof: Each d_i \geq 0, so the weighted sum is non-negative. Each d_i \leq C_i for some constant C_i \geq 1. The weighted sum is bounded by \max_i C_i. The \min(1, \cdot) operation ensures D \leq 1.

\square

C.5 Baseline-Relative Depth

Definition C.5.1 (Baseline-Relative Depth):

D_{\text{rel}}(\mathcal{T}) = \frac{D(\mathcal{T}) - D(\mathcal{T}_{\text{baseline}})}{1 - D(\mathcal{T}_{\text{baseline}})}

where \mathcal{T}_{\text{baseline}} is a minimal intervention (e.g., no structural change, no rule change, no capability change).

Proposition C.5.1: When D(\mathcal{T}_{\text{baseline}}) < 1, D_{\text{rel}}(\mathcal{T}) \in [0, 1] for \mathcal{T} with D(\mathcal{T}) \geq D(\mathcal{T}_{\text{baseline}}).

Proof: If D(\mathcal{T}) \geq D(\mathcal{T}_{\text{baseline}}), then D_{\text{rel}} \geq 0. If D(\mathcal{T}) \leq 1, then:

D_{\text{rel}} \leq \frac{1 - D(\mathcal{T}_{\text{baseline}})}{1 - D(\mathcal{T}_{\text{baseline}})} = 1

\square

