APPENDIX D

Capability Dynamics: Complete Specification

D.1 The Capability State Space

Definition D.1.1 (Capability State Space):

\mathcal{K} = \{ K \in \mathbb{R}^9 : 0 \leq k_i \leq k_i^{\max} \text{ for } i \in \{o, m, l, e, c, r, g, s, x\} \}

where k_i^{\max} are the maximum possible values for each capability component.

Proposition D.1.1: \mathcal{K} is a compact convex subset of \mathbb{R}^9.

Proof: \mathcal{K} is the product of closed intervals [0, k_i^{\max}], which is compact (by the Heine-Borel theorem) and convex (the product of convex sets is convex).

\square

D.2 The Capability Dynamics Function

Definition D.2.1 (Capability Dynamics Function):

\kappa: \mathcal{K} \times \mathcal{T} \times \mathcal{L} \times \mathcal{E} \times \Theta \rightarrow \mathcal{K}

mapping the current capability state, intervention, learning, experience, and mechanism parameter to the next capability state:

K_{t+1} = \kappa(K_t, \mathcal{T}_t, L_t, E_t, \theta_t)

D.3 The Linear Capability Dynamics Model

Assumption D.3.1 (Linear Capability Dynamics): For analytical tractability, assume:

K_{t+1} = K_t + B_L L_t + B_I \mathcal{T}_K - B_D K_t

where:

· B_L is a matrix of learning rates (how much each capability improves per unit learning)
· B_I is a matrix of investment rates (how much each capability improves per unit resource)
· B_D is a diagonal matrix of decay rates (how much each capability decays per unit time)
· \mathcal{T}_K is the capability-building portion of the intervention

Definition D.3.2 (Detailed Component-wise Dynamics):

\begin{aligned}
k_{o,t+1} &= k_{o,t} + \eta_{o,L} L_t + \eta_{o,I} \mathcal{T}_{K,o} - \eta_{o,D} k_{o,t} \\
k_{m,t+1} &= k_{m,t} + \eta_{m,L} L_t + \eta_{m,I} \mathcal{T}_{K,m} - \eta_{m,D} k_{m,t} \\
&\vdots \\
k_{x,t+1} &= k_{x,t} + \eta_{x,L} L_t + \eta_{x,I} \mathcal{T}_{K,x} - \eta_{x,D} k_{x,t}
\end{aligned}

D.4 Capability Convergence

Theorem D.4.1 (Capability Convergence): If the decay rates \eta_{i,D} > 0 for all i, and if learning and investment are bounded, then the capability state converges to a unique fixed point K^* given by:

K^* = (B_D)^{-1} (B_L L + B_I \mathcal{T}_K)

when L and \mathcal{T}_K are constant.

Proof: At the fixed point, K_{t+1} = K_t = K^*. Therefore:

K^* = K^* + B_L L + B_I \mathcal{T}_K - B_D K^*

0 = B_L L + B_I \mathcal{T}_K - B_D K^*

B_D K^* = B_L L + B_I \mathcal{T}_K

K^* = B_D^{-1} (B_L L + B_I \mathcal{T}_K)

Since B_D is diagonal with positive entries, it is invertible.

\square

D.5 Capability-Mechanism Coupling

Definition D.5.1 (Coupling Function): The coupling between capability and mechanism is:

K_{t+1} = \kappa(K_t, \mathcal{T}_t, L_t, E_t, \theta_t)

The mechanism parameter \theta_t influences capability evolution through:

1. Observation quality: \theta_t determines what can be observed, affecting k_o.
2. Model quality: \theta_t determines how the system is modeled, affecting k_m.
3. Learning efficiency: \theta_t determines how learning is incorporated, affecting k_l.

Conversely, capability influences mechanism evolution:

\theta_{t+1} = g(\theta_t, S_t, A_t, I_t, L_t)

where I_t depends on k_o and L_t depends on k_l.

