APPENDIX E

The Capability Dividend: Counterfactual Definition and Properties

E.1 The Counterfactual Framework

Definition E.1.1 (Policy): A policy \pi is a mapping from states to interventions:

\pi: \mathcal{X} \rightarrow \mathcal{T}

Definition E.1.2 (Policy with Intervention): Let \pi_{\mathcal{T}} be a policy that includes intervention \mathcal{T} at time t, and let \pi_{\mathcal{T}^0} be a comparable policy that differs only in that it does not include the capability-building component of \mathcal{T}.

E.2 The Capability Dividend

Definition E.2.1 (Capability Dividend):

\boxed{TCD(\mathcal{T}) = \mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\mathcal{T}}}\left[\sum_{\tau=t+1}^{T} \gamma^{\tau} r(X_\tau)\right] - \mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\mathcal{T}^0}}\left[\sum_{\tau=t+1}^{T} \gamma^{\tau} r(X_\tau^0)\right]}

where:

· X_\tau is the trajectory under \pi_{\mathcal{T}}
· X_\tau^0 is the trajectory under \pi_{\mathcal{T}^0}
· r(X_\tau) is the reward at state X_\tau
· \gamma \in (0,1) is the discount factor

E.3 The Marginal Capability Value

Definition E.3.1 (Marginal Capability Value):

\nabla_K V(X_t) = \frac{\partial V(X_t)}{\partial K_t}

where V(X_t) is the value function:

V(X_t) = \max_{\pi} \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\tau=t}^{T} \gamma^{\tau-t} r(X_\tau)\right]

Theorem E.3.1 (First-Order Approximation): For small capability changes \Delta K:

TCD(\mathcal{T}) \approx \nabla_K V(X_t) \cdot \Delta K(\mathcal{T})

where \Delta K(\mathcal{T}) is the capability change induced by intervention \mathcal{T}.

Proof: By Taylor expansion of the value function around K_t:

V(X_t + \Delta X) \approx V(X_t) + \nabla_K V(X_t) \cdot \Delta K

where \Delta X is the change in the augmented state due to the intervention. The Capability Dividend is the difference in future value attributable to the capability change:

TCD \approx V(S_{t+1}, K_t + \Delta K, \theta_t) - V(S_{t+1}, K_t, \theta_t)

By Taylor expansion:

V(S_{t+1}, K_t + \Delta K, \theta_t) \approx V(S_{t+1}, K_t, \theta_t) + \nabla_K V(X_t) \cdot \Delta K

Therefore:

TCD \approx \nabla_K V(X_t) \cdot \Delta K

\square

E.4 Bellman Decomposition

Theorem E.4.1 (Capability Value Emergence): The value function V(X_t) can be decomposed into state value and capability value components:

V(X_t) = V_S(S_t, \theta_t) + V_K(K_t, \theta_t) + V_{\text{coupling}}(S_t, K_t, \theta_t)

Proof: The value function satisfies the Bellman equation:

V(X_t) = \max_{\mathcal{T}_t} \left[ r(X_t, \mathcal{T}_t) + \gamma \mathbb{E}[V(X_{t+1}) | X_t, \mathcal{T}_t] \right]

The reward function can be decomposed:

r(X_t, \mathcal{T}_t) = r_S(S_t, \mathcal{T}_t) + r_K(K_t, \mathcal{T}_t) + r_{\text{coupling}}(S_t, K_t, \mathcal{T}_t)

By linearity of expectation, the value function inherits this decomposition.

\square

E.5 Conditions for Positive Capability Dividend

Theorem E.5.1 (Positive Capability Dividend): TCD(\mathcal{T}) > 0 if and only if:

\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\mathcal{T}}}\left[\sum_{\tau=t+1}^{T} \gamma^{\tau} r(X_\tau)\right] > \mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\mathcal{T}^0}}\left[\sum_{\tau=t+1}^{T} \gamma^{\tau} r(X_\tau^0)\right]

Sufficient conditions include:

1. Environmental nonstationarity: The environment changes such that higher capability enables better responses.
2. Regime shifts: The system encounters novel situations requiring structural adaptation.
3. Capability complementarity: The increased capability complements existing capabilities, producing super-additive value.

E.6 The Allocation Trade-off

Definition E.6.1 (Allocation Parameter): Let \alpha \in [0, 1] represent the fraction of intervention resources allocated to capability building:

\mathcal{T}_{\text{total}} = \mathcal{T}_S + \mathcal{T}_K

\mathcal{T}_K = \alpha \mathcal{T}_{\text{total}}, \quad \mathcal{T}_S = (1-\alpha) \mathcal{T}_{\text{total}}

Theorem E.6.1 (Optimal Allocation): The optimal allocation \alpha^* satisfies:

\frac{\partial}{\partial \alpha} \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T} \gamma^t r(X_t, \alpha)\right] \bigg|_{\alpha = \alpha^*} = 0

The first-order condition is:

\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T} \gamma^t \left( \frac{\partial r}{\partial \mathcal{T}_S} \frac{\partial \mathcal{T}_S}{\partial \alpha} + \frac{\partial r}{\partial \mathcal{T}_K} \frac{\partial \mathcal{T}_K}{\partial \alpha} \right) \right] = 0

\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T} \gamma^t \left( -\frac{\partial r}{\partial \mathcal{T}_S} \mathcal{T}_{\text{total}} + \frac{\partial r}{\partial \mathcal{T}_K} \mathcal{T}_{\text{total}} \right) \right] = 0

Therefore:

\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T} \gamma^t \frac{\partial r}{\partial \mathcal{T}_K} \right] = \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T} \gamma^t \frac{\partial r}{\partial \mathcal{T}_S} \right]

At the optimum, the marginal value of capability investment equals the marginal value of state investment.

\square

