3 Reversible Autonomous AI may execute autonomously if reversible
4 High-Depth Authorization Requires explicit multi-stakeholder authorization

F.2 The Tier-Depth Relationship

Theorem F.2.1 (Tier-Depth Monotonicity): For any intervention \mathcal{T}:

\text{Tier}(\mathcal{T}) \geq \text{Tier}(\mathcal{T}') \quad \text{if} \quad D(\mathcal{T}) \geq D(\mathcal{T}')

In other words: deeper interventions require higher governance tiers.

Proof: By definition, deeper interventions (higher D(\mathcal{T})) involve greater structural, institutional, or capability changes. These changes carry greater risk and irreversibility, requiring more stringent governance. The tier assignment is designed to reflect this.

\square

F.3 The Execution Function

Definition F.3.1 (Execution Function):

\mathcal{T}_{\text{executed}} = \text{Exec}(\mathcal{T}_{\text{proposed}}, \mathbf{P}_t, A_{\text{human}}, A_{\text{AI}})

where:

\text{Exec}(\mathcal{T}, \mathbf{P}, A_H, A_{AI}) = 
\begin{cases}
\mathcal{T} & \text{if } \mathbf{P}_{ij} = 0 \text{ and } \mathcal{T} \text{ proposed by } A_H \\
\mathcal{T} & \text{if } \mathbf{P}_{ij} = 1 \text{ and } \mathcal{T} \text{ approved by } A_H \\
\mathcal{T} & \text{if } \mathbf{P}_{ij} = 2 \text{ and } \mathcal{T} \text{ jointly executed} \\
\mathcal{T} & \text{if } \mathbf{P}_{ij} = 3 \text{ and } \mathcal{T} \text{ is reversible} \\
\mathcal{T} & \text{if } \mathbf{P}_{ij} = 4 \text{ and } \mathcal{T} \text{ authorized by stakeholders} \\
\emptyset & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

F.4 The Four-Dimensional Governance Space

Definition F.4.1 (Governance Space):

\mathcal{G} = \text{Capability} \times \text{Authority} \times \text{Accountability} \times \text{Transparency}

Definition F.4.2 (Constrained AI Action Space):

A_{AI} \subseteq A_{AI}^{\text{capability}} \cap A_{AI}^{\text{authority}} \cap A_{AI}^{\text{accountability}} \cap A_{AI}^{\text{transparency}}

Theorem F.4.1 (Separation of Agency and Authority): The constrained AI action space satisfies:

\text{Capability}(A_{AI}) > 0 \not\Rightarrow \text{Authority}(A_{AI}) > 0

Proof: The constrained action space is the intersection of the capability space and the authority space. An AI agent may have positive capability (A_{AI}^{\text{capability}} \neq \emptyset) but zero authority (A_{AI}^{\text{authority}} = \emptyset). In this case, the intersection is empty, meaning the AI cannot act despite having capability.

\square

