APPENDIX G

The Three-Layer Dynamical System: Complete Specification

G.1 The Complete Coupled System

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
S_{t+1} &= f(S_t, \mathcal{T}_t; \theta_t) + \xi_t \\
K_{t+1} &= \kappa(K_t, \mathcal{T}_t, L_t, E_t, \theta_t) \\
\theta_{t+1} &= g(\theta_t, S_t, A_t, I_t, L_t)
\end{aligned}}

G.2 The Phase Space

Definition G.2.1 (Phase Space):

\mathcal{P} = \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{K} \times \Theta

where:

· \mathcal{S} is the state space
· \mathcal{K} is the capability space
· \Theta is the mechanism parameter space

Proposition G.2.1: If \mathcal{S}, \mathcal{K}, and \Theta are all compact, then \mathcal{P} is compact.

Proof: The product of compact spaces is compact (Tychonoff's theorem).

\square

G.3 Invariant Sets

Definition G.3.1 (Invariant Set): A set \mathcal{A} \subseteq \mathcal{P} is invariant under the dynamics if:

X_t \in \mathcal{A} \Rightarrow X_{t+1} \in \mathcal{A}

Theorem G.3.1 (Existence of Invariant Set): If the dynamics are continuous and the phase space is compact, there exists at least one non-empty invariant set.

Proof: By the Brouwer fixed-point theorem applied to the map X \mapsto F(X) on the compact convex set \mathcal{P}.

\square

G.4 Stability Analysis

Definition G.4.1 (Lyapunov Stability): A fixed point X^* is Lyapunov stable if for every \varepsilon > 0, there exists \delta > 0 such that:

\|X_0 - X^*\| < \delta \Rightarrow \|X_t - X^*\| < \varepsilon \quad \forall t \geq 0

Definition G.4.2 (Asymptotic Stability): A fixed point X^* is asymptotically stable if it is Lyapunov stable and:

\lim_{t \rightarrow \infty} \|X_t - X^*\| = 0

Theorem G.4.1 (Linear Stability): Consider the linearized system:

X_{t+1} = J X_t

where J is the Jacobian of the coupled dynamics at the fixed point. The fixed point is asymptotically stable if and only if all eigenvalues of J have absolute value less than 1.

Proof: Standard result from linear dynamical systems theory.

\square

G.5 Coupling Analysis

Definition G.5.1 (Coupling Matrix): The coupling between layers is captured by the Jacobian:

J = \begin{pmatrix}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial S} & \frac{\partial f}{\partial K} & \frac{\partial f}{\partial \theta} \\
\frac{\partial \kappa}{\partial S} & \frac{\partial \kappa}{\partial K} & \frac{\partial \kappa}{\partial \theta} \\
\frac{\partial g}{\partial S} & \frac{\partial g}{\partial K} & \frac{\partial g}{\partial \theta}
\end{pmatrix}

Definition G.5.2 (Strong Coupling): The layers are strongly coupled if the off-diagonal blocks of J are non-negligible.

Definition G.5.3 (Weak Coupling): The layers are weakly coupled if the off-diagonal blocks of J are small relative to the diagonal blocks.

