APPENDIX I

Computational Simulation: Formal Specification

I.1 The Environment

Definition I.1.1 (NK Fitness Landscape): An NK fitness landscape is defined by:

· N binary genes: s = (s_1, s_2, \ldots, s_N), s_i \in \{0, 1\}
· Each gene interacts with K other genes
· The fitness is:

\Phi(s) = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \phi_i(s_i, s_{i_1}, \ldots, s_{i_K})

where \phi_i are random functions mapping \{0,1\}^{K+1} \rightarrow [0, 1].

Definition I.1.2 (Nonstationary Environment): The fitness landscape changes over time:

\Phi_t(s) = \Phi(s; \xi_t)

where \xi_t is a stochastic process controlling the landscape.

I.2 Agent Types

Definition I.2.1 (Fixed Policy Agent):

\pi_{\text{fixed}}: \mathcal{S} \rightarrow \mathcal{T}

\pi_{\text{fixed}}(s) = \mathcal{T}_0 \quad \forall s

Definition I.2.2 (Adaptive Policy Agent):

\pi_{\text{adaptive}}(s_t) = \mathcal{T}(s_t, \theta_t)

\theta_{t+1} = g(\theta_t, \text{feedback}_t)

Definition I.2.3 (Meta-Adaptive Policy Agent):

\pi_{\text{meta}}(s_t) = \mathcal{T}(s_t, \theta_t)

\theta_{t+1} = g(\theta_t, \text{feedback}_t, \phi_t)

\phi_{t+1} = \omega(\phi_t, \text{meta-feedback}_t)

Definition I.2.4 (Capability-Investing Policy Agent):

\pi_{\text{cap}}(s_t) = \mathcal{T}(s_t, \theta_t, \alpha)

where \alpha > 0 allocates resources to capability building:

K_{t+1} = \kappa(K_t, \alpha \mathcal{T}_{\text{total}}, L_t, E_t, \theta_t)

I.3 Metrics

Definition I.3.1 (Cumulative Discounted Reward):

R = \sum_{t=0}^{T} \gamma^t r(X_t, \mathcal{T}_t)

Definition I.3.2 (Capability Dividend):

TCD = \mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\text{cap}}}[R] - \mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\text{meta}}}[R]

where \pi_{\text{meta}} is the meta-adaptive policy without capability investment.

I.4 Hypothesized Results

1. Fixed Policy Degradation:

\mathbb{E}[R_{\text{fixed}}] < \mathbb{E}[R_{\text{adaptive}}]

under distribution shift.

2. Meta-Adaptive Superiority:

\mathbb{E}[R_{\text{meta}}] > \mathbb{E}[R_{\text{adaptive}}]

under regime shifts.

3. Capability Dividend Positivity:

TCD > 0

in nonstationary environments.

4. Capability Dividend Monotonicity:

\frac{\partial TCD}{\partial \text{nonstationarity}} > 0

The capability dividend increases with environmental nonstationarity.

