K.26 The First-Order vs Second-Order vs Meta-Adaptation Distinction

```
+================================================================================+
|           ORDERS OF ADAPTATION                                               |
+================================================================================+
|                                                                                |
|   +----------------------------------------------------------------+          |
|   |  FIRST-ORDER ADAPTATION                                        |          |
|   |                                                                 |          |
|   |  "Change the system"                                           |          |
|   |                                                                 |          |
|   |  S_t -> S_{t+1}                                                |          |
|   |                                                                 |          |
|   |  Example: A city builds a new transport network.                |          |
|   |                                                                 |          |
|   |  Mathematical object: State transition                          |          |
|   +----------------------------------------------------------------+          |
|                              |                                                |
|                              v                                                |
|   +----------------------------------------------------------------+          |
|   |  SECOND-ORDER ADAPTATION                                       |          |
|   |                                                                 |          |
|   |  "Change how the system changes"                                |          |
|   |                                                                 |          |
|   |  theta_t -> theta_{t+1}                                        |          |
|   |                                                                 |          |
|   |  Example: The city changes institutional rules governing        |          |
|   |           transportation, enabling new coordination modes.     |          |
|   |                                                                 |          |
|   |  Mathematical object: Mechanism parameter evolution             |          |
|   +----------------------------------------------------------------+          |
|                              |                                                |
|                              v                                                |
|   +----------------------------------------------------------------+          |
|   |  META-ADAPTATION                                               |          |
|   |                                                                 |          |
|   |  "Change how the system learns to change how it changes"        |          |
|   |                                                                 |          |
|   |  g -> g' (the update function itself changes)                  |          |
|   |                                                                 |          |
|   |  Example: The governance system creates a permanent             |          |
|   |           institutional capability for experimentation,        |          |
|   |           evaluation, learning, and revision.                  |          |
|   |                                                                 |          |
|   |  Mathematical object: Meta-update function evolution            |          |
|   +----------------------------------------------------------------+          |
|                                                                                |
+================================================================================+
```

